<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanuki Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanuki Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, corresponding to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagawa-prefecture">Kagawa Prefecture</a>. It was the birthplace of the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukai">Kūkai</a> and home of the Konpira shrine. Briefly conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Chōsokabe Motochika</a> before Hideyoshi's invasion of 1585, it was given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikoma-chikamasa">Ikoma Chikamasa</a>, who built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takamatsu">Takamatsu</a> castle from 1588; under the Tokugawa its main domain was Takamatsu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanuki-province.jpg" alt="Sanuki Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Sanuki.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sanuki bordered Awa Province to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyo-province">Iyo Province</a> to the west, and its short name was Sanshū. It belonged to the Nankaidō circuit, and the Engishiki classed it as one of the upper countries in importance and one of the middle countries in distance from the capital. Its provincial capital lay in what is now the city of Sakaide, though the exact site was identified only in 2012. The chief shrine of the province, its ichinomiya, is the Tamura jinja in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takamatsu">Takamatsu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojiki">Kojiki</a> and other early texts the area was called Iyorihiko, though the name Sanuki also appears in various spellings. The province was formed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ritsuryo">Ritsuryō</a> reforms. The Shiwaku Islands of the Seto Inland Sea were part of it from the start, while Shōdoshima and the Naoshima Islands were transferred from Bizen Province only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. The poet Sugawara no Michizane governed the province from 886 to 890. At the end of the Heian period Yashima was a stronghold of the Heike clan, who controlled the sea routes of the Inland Sea, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoshitsune">Minamoto no Yoshitsune</a> defeated them at the Battle of Yashima.</p><p>In the Muromachi period the Hosokawa clan held the province as shugo for the Ashikaga shogunate, but in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> they were eclipsed by the Miyoshi clan, who were in turn overrun by the Chōsokabe clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tosa-province">Tosa Province</a>. In the Edo period Sanuki was shared among three han, lands held directly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> and part of Tsuyama Domain. An early Meiji survey counted 395 villages with a kokudaka of 293,628 koku.</p><p>After the han were abolished, Sanuki became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagawa-prefecture">Kagawa Prefecture</a> in 1872. It was merged the next year with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima-prefecture">Tokushima Prefecture</a> and Awaji into Myōdō Prefecture, separated on 5 September 1875, joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehime-prefecture">Ehime Prefecture</a> on 21 August 1876 and separated for good on 3 December 1888.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sanuki Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In one of the battles of the Genpei War, fought in <b>Sanuki Province</b> on Shikoku, near present-day Takamatsu, the forces of the Taira clan were defeated by the Minamoto.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-22">22 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He overran Awa, <b>Sanuki</b> and Iyo and by 1585 controlled most of Shikoku.</blockquote><a href="/state/chosokabe-clan-domain">Chosokabe clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Ikoma Chikamasa received <b>Sanuki</b> from Toyotomi Hideyoshi in 1587 and in 1588 began Takamatsu castle, a sea castle with moats fed by seawater.</blockquote><a href="/state/takamatsu-domain">Takamatsu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
